phical methods may involve residual plots, where the residuals (the differences between observed and predicted values) are plotted against fitted values or independent variables. This visualization helps to identify patterns that may indicate model inadequacies, such as non-linearity or heteroscedasticity (non-constant variance of errors).\n\nStatistical tests, such as the Durbin-Watson test for autocorrelation or the Breusch-Pagan test for heteroscedasticity, are also commonly used to assess model adequacy. These tests provide formal criteria for evaluating whether the assumptions of the regression model hold true. For instance, the Durbin-Watson statistic tests for the presence of autocorrelation in the residuals, which, if present, suggests that the model may be missing key explanatory variables or that the functional form is incorrect.\n\nThe assumptions underlying model adequacy checking are crucial for the validity of the model. Key assumptions include linearity, independence, homoscedasticity, and normality of residuals. Linearity assumes that the relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear. Independence requires that the residuals are uncorrelated with one another. Homoscedasticity assumes that the variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables, while normality posits that the residuals are normally distributed.\n\nViolations of these assumptions can lead to biased estimates, inefficient predictions, and invalid statistical inferences. For example, if the assumption of homoscedasticity is violated, standard errors may be underestimated, leading to overly optimistic confidence intervals and hypothesis tests. Similarly, if the residuals are not normally distributed, the validity of t-tests and F-tests may be compromised.\n\nIn conclusion, model adequacy checking is an integral part of the regression analysis process, ensuring that the statistical model used is appropriate for the data at hand. By